// @check
// @error member access '.n' on non-object
// THE UN-NARROW, AND IT IS A SOUNDNESS CONJUNCT — measured, not asserted.
//
// An assignment narrowing is a fact about a PATH, and a later write of null ends
// that path. `assign-narrow-then-null-guard.vl` makes the write below legal again
// (it was "cannot assign null to {n: i32}"); this file pins what must happen to the
// binding's type when it lands. Restoring the declared `S | null` turns the read
// into the clean reject above.
//
// WITHOUT the restore the same three lines type-check with NO diagnostic at all and
// TRAP at runtime — `wasm trap: null reference` — because every read after the
// write still sees the non-null narrowing. That is the difference between a
// diagnostic and a miscompile, and no file in the 1,508-file corpus distinguishes
// them: the sabotage that drops the restore reads 0 corpus rows and moves neither
// of this slice's other two nullable fixtures. It had to be built by hand.
type S = { n: i32 }

let v: S | null = null
v = { n: 7 }
v = null
print(v.n)
